# ThreadSanitizer suppressions (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=tests/tsan.supp).
# CI's ThreadSanitizer job leaves the window tests out (ctest -LE window);
# these rules are what it takes to run them too, as far as it is known.
#
# Qt is not built with ThreadSanitizer, so the synchronization inside it is
# invisible, and memory Qt passes between its own threads (its thread pool
# drawing icons, a wait condition torn down at exit) looks raced. These rules
# cover only what Qt does inside itself: Horizon's own workers are
# std::threads, and a race in Horizon's code is still reported.

# A dynamically linked Qt (a distribution's): ignore what Qt's GUI libraries
# do inside themselves. Not QtCore: ignoring it hides Qt's thread starts and
# exits from ThreadSanitizer's thread registry, which then fails its own
# check when a later std::thread reuses a thread id.
called_from_lib:libQt6Gui.so
called_from_lib:libQt6Widgets.so
called_from_lib:libQt6OpenGL.so
called_from_lib:libQt6OpenGLWidgets.so

# QtCore's thread pool and wait conditions (by name, which a static Qt, as in
# CI, keeps too).
race:QThreadPoolThread
race:QThreadPool
race:QWaitCondition

# Qt's pool threads are still running when the process exits.
thread:QThread
